@subsection The Core of the Evaluator

The evaluation process can be described as the interaction between two
procedures: @code{evaluate} and @code{applyProcedure}.

@subsubheading Eval

@code{Evaluate} takes as arguments an expression and an environment.  It
classifies the expression and directs its evaluation.  @code{Evaluate} is
structured as a case
analysis of the syntactic type of the expression to be evaluated.  In order to
keep the procedure general, we express the determination of the type of an
expression abstractly, making no commitment to any particular representation
for the various types of expressions.  Each type of expression has a predicate
that tests for it and an abstract means for selecting its parts.  This
@newterm{abstract syntax} makes it easy to see how we can change the syntax of
the language by using the same evaluator, but with a different collection of
syntax procedures.

@noindent
@b{Primitive expressions}

@itemize @bullet

@item
For self-evaluating expressions, such as numbers, strings, and booleans,
@code{evaluate} returns the expression's value.

@item
@code{Evaluate} must look up variables in the environment to find their values.

@item
An array or object literal is structured data: @code{evaluate} computes its
element or field expressions in source order and constructs the corresponding
guest array or record value.

@end itemize

@noindent
@b{Special forms}

@itemize @bullet

@item
A @code{const} or @code{let} declaration recursively calls @code{evaluate} to
compute the initial value and installs a binding in the current scope.  The
environment must be modified to create the binding of the variable.

@item
An assignment to a variable must recursively call
@code{evaluate} to compute the new value to be associated with the variable.  The
environment must be modified to change the binding of the variable.

@item
An @code{if} statement requires special processing of its parts, so as to
execute the consequent if the test is true, and otherwise to execute the
alternative.  A conditional expression and a @code{switch} statement likewise
select exactly one branch rather than evaluating every branch.

@item
A function expression must be transformed into an applicable procedure by
packaging together the parameters and body specified by the function
expression with the environment of the evaluation.

@item
A block requires evaluating its sequence of statements in
the order in which they appear.

@item
A @code{while} or @code{for}-@code{of} statement requires special processing
of its parts: the loop condition is evaluated before each turn, and a
@code{for}-@code{of} walks the elements of an array in order.  A @code{return}
completes the enclosing call abruptly with the value of its operand.

@end itemize

@noindent
@b{Combinations}

@itemize @bullet

@item
For a procedure application, @code{evaluate} must recursively evaluate the
operator part and the operands of the combination.  The resulting procedure
and arguments are passed to @code{applyProcedure}, which handles the actual
procedure application.

@end itemize

@noindent
Here is the definition of @code{evaluate}:

@example typescript
export const evaluate = (expr: Expr, env: Env): Outcome => @{
  switch (expr.tag) @{
    case "number":
    case "string":
    case "boolean":
      return ok(expr.value);
    case "null":
      return ok(null);
    case "undefined":
      return ok(undefined);
    case "variable": @{
      const cell = findCell(env, expr.name);
      if (cell === undefined || !cell.initialized) @{
        return fail(@{ tag: "unbound-name", name: expr.name @});
      @}
      return ok(cell.value);
    @}
    case "template":
      return evalTemplate(expr, env);
    case "array":
      return evalArray(expr, env);
    case "object":
      return evalObject(expr, env);
    case "unary": @{
      const operand = evaluate(expr.operand, env);
      if (operand.tag !== "ok") @{
        return operand;
      @}
      return applyUnaryOperation(expr.op, operand.value);
    @}
    case "binary": @{
      const left = evaluate(expr.left, env);
      if (left.tag !== "ok") @{
        return left;
      @}
      const right = evaluate(expr.right, env);
      if (right.tag !== "ok") @{
        return right;
      @}
      return applyBinaryOperation(expr.op, left.value, right.value);
    @}
    case "logical":
      return evalLogical(expr, env);
    case "conditional": @{
      const test = evaluate(expr.test, env);
      if (test.tag !== "ok") @{
        return test;
      @}
      if (typeof test.value !== "boolean") @{
        return fail(
            @{ tag: "bad-operand",
            operator: "?:",
            detail: "test is not a boolean" @}
          );
      @}
      return evaluate(
        test.value ? expr.consequent : expr.alternative,
        env);
    @}
    case "assign":
      return evalAssignment(expr, env);
    case "arrow":
      return ok(makeProcedure(expr.params, expr.body, env));
    case "call":
      return evalCall(expr, env);
    case "member":
    case "index":
      return memberGet(expr, env);
    case "new-error":
    case "new-map":
    case "new-set":
      return evalArray(expr, env);
    case "delay":
    case "force":
    case "choose":
    case "require":
    case "ramb":
      return fail(@{ tag: "unknown-syntax", construct: expr.tag @});
  @}
@};
@end example

@noindent
The last cases are deliberate: the nodes @code{delay}, @code{force},
@code{choose}, @code{require}, and @code{ramb} belong to the named lazy and
search experiments of @ref{4.2} and @ref{4.3}, are rejected by the core
checker, and are reported here as @code{unknown-syntax} rather than given a
hidden lazy or search semantics.  The core
language has no hidden lazy or nondeterministic behavior.

@noindent
For clarity, @code{evaluate} has been implemented as a case analysis: an
exhaustive @code{switch} on the node tag of the shared syntax union, so a
new kind of node must be added to the union and to this dispatch together.
The disadvantage
of this is that our procedure handles only the node kinds we have declared,
and no new ones can be defined without editing the definition of
@code{evaluate}.  In most language implementations, dispatching on the type of an
expression is done in a data-directed style.  This allows a user to add new
types of expressions that @code{evaluate} can distinguish, without modifying
the definition of @code{evaluate} itself.  (See @ref{Exercise 4.3}.)

@noindent
Every branch answers the typed @code{Outcome}: @w{@code{@{ tag: "ok", value
@}}} carries the computed @code{Value}, and @w{@code{@{ tag: "error", error
@}}} carries one of the @code{GuestError} variants.  The errors are data, not
thrown host exceptions; a failed lookup or a bad operand returns immediately,
and no later effect of that evaluation runs.

@noindent
The named handlers behind the remaining branches are small and follow the
same pattern.  @code{evalTemplate} evaluates the embedded expressions in
order and concatenates the chunks with them.  @code{evalArray} and
@code{evalObject} evaluate elements and field values in source order (spreads
contribute items, as in @code{evalArgs}) and construct an
@code{ArrayValue} or @code{RecordValue}.  @code{applyUnary} and
@code{applyBinary} check their operands before combining them---numbers for
arithmetic, same-type operands for @code{+}, booleans for comparisons---and
answer @code{bad-operand} otherwise.  @code{evalLogical} short-circuits over
booleans.  @code{evalCall} is the case shown below; @code{memberGet} reads a
named field or computed index from a record, array, map, or set, answering
@code{unknown-field} for a missing name; @code{evalArray} builds the
boundary-only @code{Error}, @code{Map}, and @code{Set} values.  Each of these
returns at the first failed sub-evaluation, so a rejected operand never
produces a mutation or a transcript line.

@subsubheading Apply

The procedure @code{applyProcedure} takes two arguments, a procedure and an
array of arguments to which the procedure should be applied.  It classifies
procedures into two kinds: it calls @code{applyPrimitiveProcedure} to apply
primitives; it applies closures by sequentially executing the
statements that make up the body of the procedure.  The environment for the execution of the
body of a closure is constructed by extending the base environment
carried by the closure to include a frame that binds the parameters of the
closure (and its rest name, if it has one) to the arguments to which the
closure is to be applied.  Here is
the definition of @code{applyProcedure}:

@example typescript
export const applyProcedure = (
  procedure: Value,
  args: Value[]
): Outcome => @{
  if (isPrimitive(procedure)) @{
    return applyPrimitiveProcedure(procedure, args);
  @}
  if (isClosure(procedure)) @{
    const frame = extendEnvironment(procedure, args);
    if (frame.tag !== "ok") @{
      return frame;
    @}
    const completion = evalSequence(procedure.body.body, frame.value);
    if (completion.tag === "normal" || completion.tag === "return") @{
      return ok(completion.value);
    @}
    if (completion.tag === "throw") @{
      return fail(@{ tag: "guest-throw", value: completion.value @});
    @}
    return fail(
        @{ tag: "bad-operand",
        operator: completion.tag,
        detail: "outside its construct" @}
      );
  @}
  return fail(@{ tag: "not-callable", detail: format(procedure) @});
@};
@end example

@noindent
The @code{completion} value is the statement-level result described under
@code{evalSequence} below: a body can finish normally, return a value, throw,
or attempt a @code{break} or @code{return} that leaves its construct.  Only
the first two become the value of the call; an uncaught guest throw becomes
the @code{guest-throw} error, and a stray @code{break} or @code{continue} is
reported rather than smuggled through as a value.

@subsubheading Procedure arguments

When @code{evaluate} processes a procedure application, it uses
@code{evalArgs} to produce the array of arguments to which the procedure
is to be applied.  The procedure @code{evalArgs} takes as an argument the
argument nodes of the call.  It evaluates each one from left to right---the
order the guest language fixes---and returns the corresponding values; a
spread argument contributes the items of its array:@footnote{We could have
simplified the @code{call}
case in @code{evaluate} by using @code{map} over the arguments rather than
writing an explicit @code{evalArgs} procedure.  We chose not to use
@code{map} here to emphasize the fact that the evaluator's own recursion is
written out by hand, even though the language that it supports and the
language that implements it both include higher-order procedures.}

@example typescript
export const evalArgs = (
  args: ReadonlyArray<Arg>,
  env: Env,
): Outcome => @{
  const values: Value[] = [];
  for (const arg of args) @{
    const value = evaluate(arg.expr, env);
    if (value.tag !== "ok") @{
      return value;
    @}
    if (arg.kind === "item") @{
      values.push(value.value);
    @} else if (
      value.value !== null &&
      typeof value.value === "object" &&
      value.value.tag === "array"
    ) @{
      for (const item of value.value.items) @{
        values.push(item);
      @}
    @} else @{
      return fail(
          @{ tag: "bad-operand",
          operator: "spread",
          detail: "spread of a non-array" @}
        );
    @}
  @}
  return ok(values);
@};
@end example

@subsubheading Conditionals

The procedure @code{evalIf} executes the @code{if} statement in the given
environment.  It evaluates the test part; if the result is true, @code{evalIf}
executes the consequent, otherwise it executes the alternative.  The
conditional expression of @code{evaluate} above is the expression-level twin:
it computes exactly one arm and answers that arm's value.

@example typescript
export const evalIf = (stmt: Stmt, env: Env): Completion => @{
  const predicate = evaluate(stmt.test, env);
  if (predicate.tag !== "ok") @{
    return @{ tag: "error", error: predicate.error @};
  @}
  if (typeof predicate.value !== "boolean") @{
    return @{
      tag: "error",
      error: @{ tag: "bad-operand",
        operator: "if",
        detail: "test is not a boolean" @},
    @};
  @}
  if (predicate.value) @{
    return execStatement(stmt.consequent, env);
  @}
  return stmt.alternative === null
    ? @{ tag: "normal", value: undefined @}
    : execStatement(stmt.alternative, env);
@};
@end example

@noindent
The type test in @code{evalIf} highlights the issue of the
connection between an implemented language and an implementation language.  The
@code{test} is evaluated in the language being implemented and thus
yields a value in that language.  The interpreter must translate that value
into the decision the @code{if} of
the implementation language needs: The metacircular representation of truth might not be
the same as that of the implementation language.  Here the guest language
settles the question statically---a condition must be a @code{boolean}, and
the subset checker answers @code{ExpectedBoolean} when it can see
otherwise---but the evaluator still checks, because an externally built
syntax tree can lie.@footnote{In the book, the
language being implemented and the implementation language are the same, which
is what makes the question vertiginous.  Here they are not: the evaluated
truth values are the guest booleans @code{true} and @code{false}, and
the test above answers the host boolean that the conditional needs.
Contemplation of the meaning of this check here yields expansion of
consciousness without the abuse of substance.}

@subsubheading Sequences

The procedure @code{evalSequence} is used by @code{applyProcedure} to execute
the statements of a procedure body and by @code{execute} to
execute the statements of a block.  It takes
as arguments a sequence of statements and declarations and an environment, and
executes
the statements in the order in which they occur.  Statement execution answers
a @code{Completion}: normal execution carries the value of the last statement,
and a @code{return}, @code{break}, @code{continue}, or @code{throw} completes
the enclosing construct abruptly.@footnote{An empty sequence has no final
statement.
The book's @code{evalSequence} would chase @code{restExps} forever; the
edition's @code{evalSequence} answers an empty sequence's normal completion
with @code{undefined} instead.}

@example typescript
export type Completion =
  | @{ tag: "normal"; value: Value @}
  | @{ tag: "return"; value: Value @}
  | @{ tag: "break" @}
  | @{ tag: "continue" @}
  | @{ tag: "throw"; value: Value @}
  | @{ tag: "error"; error: GuestError @};

export const evalSequence = (
  statements: ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt>,
  env: Env,
): Completion => @{
  let result: Completion = @{
    tag: "normal",
    value: undefined,
  @};
  for (const statement of statements) @{
    result = execItem(statement, env);
    if (result.tag !== "normal") @{
      return result;
    @}
  @}
  return result;
@};
@end example

@noindent
Statement execution is the companion dispatcher, and it is the one place the
two halves of the shared syntax meet: declarations go to
@code{evalDeclaration}, the @code{if} statement to @code{evalIf}, a block to
@code{evalSequence}, and the case analysis is executed by the transformation
of @ref{4.1.2}, just as the book's @code{cond} is.

@example typescript
const execItem = (item: Decl | Stmt, env: Env): Completion => @{
  switch (item.tag) @{
    case "var-decl":
    case "function-decl":
    case "type-decl":
    case "interface-decl":
    case "import":
      return evalDeclaration(item, env);
    default:
      return execStatement(item, env);
  @}
@};

const execStatement = (statement: Stmt, env: Env): Completion => @{
  switch (statement.tag) @{
    case "expr-stmt": @{
      const value = evaluate(statement.expr, env);
      return value.tag === "ok"
        ? @{ tag: "normal", value: value.value @}
        : @{ tag: "error", error: value.error @};
    @}
    case "block":
      return evalSequence(statement.body, env);
    case "if":
      return evalIf(statement, env);
    case "while":
      return execWhile(statement, env);
    case "for-of":
      return execForOf(statement, env);
    case "switch":
      return execStatement(switchToIf(statement), env);
    case "return":
      return execReturn(statement, env);
    case "break":
      return @{ tag: "break" @};
    case "continue":
      return @{ tag: "continue" @};
    case "throw": @{
      const thrown = evaluate(statement.argument, env);
      return thrown.tag === "ok"
        ? @{ tag: "throw", value: thrown.value @}
        : @{ tag: "error", error: thrown.error @};
    @}
    case "try":
      return execTry(statement, env);
  @}
@};
@end example

@noindent
The @code{while} and @code{for}-@code{of} cases repeat their body under the
loop rule of the grammar: the test before each turn, the array's elements in
order, and @code{break}/@code{continue} completing the iteration rather than
the call.  @code{try} and @code{throw} are admitted in guest source only at
the host I/O and process boundary; the evaluator implements their completion
semantics, but the teaching kernel itself contains no @code{try}.

@subsubheading Assignments and declarations

The following procedure handles assignments to variables.  It calls
@code{evaluate} to find the value to be assigned and transmits the variable
and the resulting value to @code{setVariableValue} to be installed in the
designated environment.

@example typescript
export const evalAssignment = (expr: Expr, env: Env): Outcome => @{
  const value = evaluate(expr.value, env);
  if (value.tag !== "ok") @{
    return value;
  @}
  const target = expr.target;
  const installed =
    target.tag === "variable"
      ? setVariableValue(target.name, value.value, env)
      : memberSet(target, value.value, env);
  if (installed.tag !== "ok") @{
    return installed;
  @}
  return ok(value.value);
@};
@end example

@noindent
An assignment to a member or an index is not a name rebinding: the branch
calls @code{memberSet}, the writing twin of the @code{memberGet} case of
@ref{4.1.1}, which stores into the guest record, array, map, or set the
target denotes.

@noindent
Declarations of variables are handled in a similar manner.@footnote{This
implementation of declarations ignores a subtle issue in the handling of
internal definitions, although it works correctly in most cases.  We will see
what the problem is and how to solve it in @ref{4.1.6}.}

@example typescript
export const evalDeclaration = (decl: Decl, env: Env): Completion => @{
  if (decl.tag === "var-decl") @{
    const value = evaluate(decl.init, env);
    if (value.tag !== "ok") @{
      return @{ tag: "error", error: value.error @};
    @}
    return install(decl.name, value.value, decl.kind === "let", env);
  @}
  if (decl.tag === "function-decl") @{
    return install(
        decl.name,
        makeProcedure(decl.params, decl.body, env),
        true,
        env
      );
  @}
  if (decl.tag === "import") @{
    const error = linkImport(decl, env);
    return error === null
      ? @{ tag: "normal", value: undefined @}
      : @{ tag: "error", error: error @};
  @}
  return @{ tag: "normal", value: undefined @};
@};

const install = (
  name: string,
  value: Value,
  mutable: boolean,
  env: Env
): Completion => @{
  const installed = defineVariableValue(name, value, mutable, env);
  return installed.tag === "ok"
    ? @{ tag: "normal", value: undefined @}
    : @{ tag: "error", error: installed.error @};
@};
@end example

@noindent
Type declarations bind nothing at run time: they are erased by the checker.
Imports erase their type-only names the same way; each value name is bound
from the host's linked-module table, so no module is loaded inside the
evaluator kernel.

@noindent
An assignment expression answers the value that was assigned, and a
declaration is a statement whose completion carries
@code{undefined}.@footnote{As we said when we introduced
declarations and assignment, the value of a definition is
implementation-dependent in many languages; this edition follows the guest
language's own rule, where assignment is an expression and declaration is
not.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.1}Exercise 4.1:} In this metacircular evaluator we
can tell how operands are evaluated, because the guest language specifies the
order and the evaluator writes it out: @code{evalArgs} walks its arguments
from left to right, and the pinned host runtime evaluates the calls it makes
in that same specified order.  The book's puzzle was about an order inherited
without a specification; ours is about making a specified order explicit and
observable.

Write a version of @code{evalArgs} that evaluates operands from left to
right regardless of any surrounding convention.  Also write a version of
@code{evalArgs} that evaluates operands
from right to left.  Show the two versions an argument list whose operands
append distinct labels to a transcript array, and confirm that only the
left-to-right version agrees with the guest language's evaluation order.
@end quotation
@node	4.1.2, 4.1.3, 4.1.1, 4.1
@subsection Representing Expressions

The evaluator is reminiscent of the symbolic differentiation program discussed
in @ref{2.3.2}.  Both programs operate on symbolic expressions.  In
both programs, the result of operating on a compound expression is determined
by operating recursively on the pieces of the expression and combining the
results in a way that depends on the type of the expression.  In both programs
we used data abstraction to decouple the general rules of operation from the
details of how expressions are represented.  In the differentiation program
this meant that the same differentiation procedure could deal with algebraic
expressions in prefix form, in infix form, or in some other form.  For the
evaluator, this means that the syntax of the language being evaluated is
determined solely by the procedures that classify and extract pieces of
expressions.

Here is the specification of the syntax of our language:

@itemize

@item
The shared syntax is one tagged union, used by the reader, both teaching
evaluators, the explicit-control evaluator, and the compiler.  Every node also
records its source span (@code{Span@{start, end, line, column@}}), omitted
here for brevity.  The expression nodes are:

@example typescript
type Expr =
  | @{ tag: "number"; value: number @}
  | @{ tag: "string"; value: string @}
  | @{ tag: "boolean"; value: boolean @}
  | @{ tag: "null" @}
  | @{ tag: "undefined" @}
  | @{ tag: "template";
    chunks: ReadonlyArray<string>;
    exprs: ReadonlyArray<Expr> @}
  | @{ tag: "variable"; name: string @}
  | @{ tag: "array"; elements: ReadonlyArray<Arg> @}
  | @{ tag: "object";
    fields: ReadonlyArray<@{ key: string; value: Expr @}> @}
  | @{ tag: "unary"; op: "!" | "+" | "-" | "typeof"; operand: Expr @}
  | @{ tag: "binary";
      op: "+" | "-" | "*" | "/" | "%" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">="
        | "===" | "!==";
      left: Expr;
      right: Expr @}
  | @{ tag: "logical"; op: "&&" | "||"; left: Expr; right: Expr @}
  | @{ tag: "conditional";
    test: Expr;
    consequent: Expr;
    alternative: Expr @}
  | @{ tag: "assign"; target: Expr; value: Expr @}
  | @{ tag: "arrow"; params: ReadonlyArray<Param>; body: Block @}
  | @{ tag: "call"; callee: Expr; args: ReadonlyArray<Arg> @}
  | @{ tag: "member"; object: Expr; name: string @}
  | @{ tag: "index"; object: Expr; index: Expr @}
  | @{ tag: "new-error"; args: ReadonlyArray<Expr> @}
  | @{ tag: "new-map"; args: ReadonlyArray<Expr> @}
  | @{ tag: "new-set"; args: ReadonlyArray<Expr> @};

type Arg = @{ kind: "item"; expr: Expr @}
  | @{ kind: "spread"; expr: Expr @};
@end example

@noindent
The only self-evaluating items are numbers, strings, booleans, and the two
empty literals:

@example typescript
export const isSelfEvaluating = (expr: Expr): boolean =>
  expr.tag === "number" ||
  expr.tag === "string" ||
  expr.tag === "boolean" ||
  expr.tag === "null" ||
  expr.tag === "undefined";
@end example

@item
Variables are @code{variable} nodes carrying a name, and nothing else is a
variable:

@example typescript
export const isVariable = (
  expr: Expr
): boolean => expr.tag === "variable";

export const variableName = (expr: Expr): string | undefined =>
  expr.tag === "variable" ? expr.name : undefined;
@end example

@item
There is no quotation form.  A program that means to use data writes the data
as an array or object literal, and symbolic data is constructed from typed
values---strings, records, and the recursive pair constructors of
@ref{4.1.3}---rather than read as quoted text.  The syntax layer exposes a
literal's parts:

@example typescript
export const arrayElements = (
  expr: Expr
): ReadonlyArray<Arg> | undefined =>
  expr.tag === "array" ? expr.elements : undefined;

export const objectFields = (
  expr: Expr,
): ReadonlyArray<@{ key: string; value: Expr @}> | undefined =>
  expr.tag === "object" ? expr.fields : undefined;
@end example

@item
Assignments have the form @code{@var{target} = @var{value}}, where the target
is a variable, a member access, or an index access:

@example typescript
export const isAssignment = (
  expr: Expr
): boolean => expr.tag === "assign";

export const assignmentTarget = (expr: Expr): Expr | undefined =>
  expr.tag === "assign" ? expr.target : undefined;

export const assignmentValue = (expr: Expr): Expr | undefined =>
  expr.tag === "assign" ? expr.value : undefined;
@end example

@item
Declarations introduce bindings.  A variable declaration carries a kind
(@code{const} or @code{let}), a name, an optional declared type, and an
initializer; a function declaration is sugar for a @code{const} binding of a
function expression:

@example typescript
readAll(`
  const ⟨@var{var}⟩: ⟨@var{type}⟩ = ⟨@var{init}⟩;
`)
@end example

@noindent
or the form

@example typescript
readAll(`
  function ⟨@var{name}⟩(
    ⟨@var{param₁}⟩: ⟨@var{type₁}⟩,
    @r{…},
    ⟨@var{paramₙ}⟩: ⟨@var{typeₙ}⟩): ⟨@var{type}⟩ @{
    ⟨@var{body}⟩
  @}
`)
@end example

The latter form is syntactic sugar for

@example typescript
readAll(`
  const ⟨@var{name}⟩ = (
    ⟨@var{param₁}⟩: ⟨@var{type₁}⟩,
    @r{…},
    ⟨@var{paramₙ}⟩: ⟨@var{typeₙ}⟩): ⟨@var{type}⟩ => @{
    ⟨@var{body}⟩
  @};
`)
@end example

The corresponding syntax procedures are the following (a function
declaration's value is the closure its parameter list and body build, so
@code{declarationValue} answers the initializer of a variable declaration and
nothing for a function declaration):

@example typescript
export const isDeclaration = (node: Decl | Stmt): boolean =>
  node.tag === "var-decl" || node.tag === "function-decl";

export const declarationName = (
  node: Decl | Stmt
): string | undefined =>
  node.tag === "var-decl" || node.tag === "function-decl"
    ? node.name
    : undefined;

export const declarationValue = (
  node: Decl | Stmt,
): Expr | undefined =>
  node.tag === "var-decl" ? node.init : undefined;

export const isConst = (node: Decl | Stmt): boolean =>
  node.tag === "var-decl" && node.kind === "const";
@end example

@item
A function expression is an @code{arrow} node carrying a parameter list and a
body block.  A parameter is either an ordinary named parameter with an
optional type, or a final rest parameter that collects the remaining
arguments:

@example typescript
type Param =
  | @{ kind: "param";
    name: string;
    optional: boolean;
    type: TypeNode | null @}
  | @{ kind: "rest"; name: string; type: TypeNode @};

export const isArrow = (expr: Expr): boolean =>
  expr.tag === "arrow";

export const arrowParameters = (
  expr: Expr
): ReadonlyArray<Param> | undefined =>
  expr.tag === "arrow" ? expr.params : undefined;

export const arrowBody = (expr: Expr): Block | undefined =>
  expr.tag === "arrow" ? expr.body : undefined;
@end example

We also provide a constructor for the procedure value an arrow or a function
declaration denotes, which is used by
@code{evalDeclaration}, above:

@example typescript
export const makeProcedure = (
  params: ReadonlyArray<Param>,
  body: Block,
  env: Env,
): Value => @{
  const names: string[] = [];
  let rest: string | null = null;
  for (const param of params) @{
    if (param.kind === "param") @{
      names.push(param.name);
    @} else @{
      rest = param.name;
    @}
  @}
  return @{ tag: "closure", params: names, rest, body, env @};
@};
@end example

@item
Conditionals come in two shapes: the @code{if} statement, whose alternative is
optional, and the conditional expression, which has a test, a consequent, and
an alternative.@footnote{An @code{if} statement has no value to be
unspecified: it executes exactly one of its branches.  A conditional
expression requires both arms.  In both forms the test must be a
@code{boolean}; the subset checker answers @code{ExpectedBoolean} when it can
see otherwise.  The guest language's booleans are the literals @code{true} and
@code{false}, and the environment of @ref{4.1.4} binds nothing by that name.}

@example typescript
export const isIf = (node: Stmt): boolean => node.tag === "if";

export const ifTest = (node: Stmt): Expr | undefined =>
  node.tag === "if" ? node.test : undefined;

export const ifConsequent = (node: Stmt): Stmt | undefined =>
  node.tag === "if" ? node.consequent : undefined;

export const ifAlternative = (node: Stmt): Stmt | undefined =>
  node.tag === "if" && node.alternative !== null
    ? node.alternative
    : undefined;
@end example

We also provide a constructor for @code{if} statements, to be used by
@code{switchToIf} to transform a case analysis into a nest of @code{if}
statements:

@example typescript
// Every constructed node carries the Span of the source it derives
// from.
export const makeIf = (
  test: Expr,
  consequent: Stmt,
  alternative: Stmt,
  span: Span
): Stmt =>
  (@{ tag: "if", test, consequent, alternative, span @});
@end example

@item
A block packages a sequence of statements and declarations into a single
statement.  The statement nodes are the block, the loops, the abrupt
completions, and the statement wrapper for an expression:

@example typescript
type Stmt =
  | @{ tag: "block"; body: ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt> @}
  | @{ tag: "if";
    test: Expr;
    consequent: Stmt;
    alternative: Stmt | null @}
  | @{ tag: "while"; test: Expr; body: Stmt @}
  | @{ tag: "for-of"; name: string; iterable: Expr; body: Stmt @}
  | @{ tag: "switch";
      discriminant: Expr;
      cases: ReadonlyArray<
        @{ test: Expr; body: ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt> @}>;
      defaultBody: ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt> | null @}
  | @{ tag: "return"; argument: Expr | null @}
  | @{ tag: "break" @}
  | @{ tag: "continue" @}
  | @{ tag: "throw"; argument: Expr @}
  | @{ tag: "expr-stmt"; expr: Expr @};
@end example

@noindent
We include selectors that return the
first statement and the rest of the statements in the
sequence.@footnote{These selectors for a sequence of statements, and the
corresponding ones for a list of arguments, are not intended as a data
abstraction.  They are introduced as mnemonic names for the basic array
operations in order to make it easier to understand the explicit-control
evaluator in @ref{5.4}.}

@example typescript
export const isLastExp = (
  seq: ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt>,
): boolean =>
  seq.length <= 1;

export const firstExp = (
  seq: ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt>
): Decl | Stmt | undefined =>
  seq.length === 0 ? undefined : seq[0];

export const restExps = (
  seq: ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt>
): ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt> =>
  seq.slice(1);
@end example

We also include a constructor @code{sequenceToExp} that transforms a sequence
of statements into a single statement, using a block if necessary:

@example typescript
export const sequenceToExp = (
  seq: ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt>,
  span: Span
): Stmt => @{
  if (seq.length === 1) @{
    const only = seq[0];
    if (only !== undefined && only.tag !== "var-decl"
      && only.tag !== "function-decl") @{
      return only;
    @}
  @}
  return @{ tag: "block", body: seq, span @};
@};
@end example

@item
A procedure application is a @code{call} node.  The callee of the expression
is the operator part, and the args are the operands:

@example typescript
export const isApplication = (
  expr: Expr
): boolean => expr.tag === "call";

export const operator = (expr: Expr): Expr | undefined =>
  expr.tag === "call" ? expr.callee : undefined;

export const operands = (
  expr: Expr,
): ReadonlyArray<Arg> | undefined =>
  expr.tag === "call" ? expr.args : undefined;

export const noOperands = (
  ops: ReadonlyArray<Arg>
): boolean => ops.length === 0;

export const firstOperand = (
  ops: ReadonlyArray<Arg>
): Expr | undefined => @{
  const first = ops[0];
  return first === undefined ? undefined : first.expr;
@};

export const restOperands = (
  ops: ReadonlyArray<Arg>
): ReadonlyArray<Arg> => ops.slice(1);
@end example

@end itemize

@subsubheading Derived expressions

Some special forms in our language can be defined in terms of statements
involving other special forms, rather than being implemented directly.  One
example is the case analysis @code{switch}, which can be implemented as a nest
of @code{if}
statements.  For example, we can reduce the problem of executing the
statement

@example typescript
readAll(`
  switch (x) @{
    case 0:
      return "zero";
    case 1:
    case 2:
      return "small";
    default:
      return "large";
  @}
`)
@end example

@noindent
to the problem of executing the following statement involving only @code{if}
statements and returns:

@example typescript
switchToIf(sequenceToExp(readAll(`
  switch (x) @{
    case 0:
      return "zero";
    case 1:
    case 2:
      return "small";
    default:
      return "large";
  @}
`)))
// => one block statement that binds the discriminant once, then nests
// ifs:
//   const @@switch-value = x;
//   if (@@switch-value === 0) @{ return "zero"; @}
//   else if (@@switch-value === 1 ||
//            @@switch-value === 2) @{ return "small"; @}
//   else @{ return "large"; @}
@end example

@noindent
Implementing the evaluation of a case analysis in this way simplifies the
evaluator
because it reduces the number of special forms for which the evaluation process
must be explicitly specified.  The guest language pins the details the
transformation must respect: the discriminant is computed once (so the
transformation binds it first), consecutive empty case labels fall through to
one shared body, and every non-empty case body completes abruptly with
@code{return}, @code{throw}, or @code{break}, so a trailing @code{break} is
the end of the branch and nothing more.

We include syntax procedures that extract the parts of a @code{switch}
statement, and a procedure @code{switchToIf} that transforms a case analysis
into @code{if}
statements.  A case analysis has a discriminant, a list of test-body cases,
and an optional default body.@footnote{When no case matches and there is no
default body, the statement simply completes normally with no value; there is
no unspecified result, because a statement has no result value.}  The
transformation below shows the one-label-per-case shape; the engine's version
also groups consecutive empty labels and strips a trailing @code{break}.

@example typescript
export const isSwitch = (node: Stmt): boolean => node.tag === "switch";

export const switchDiscriminant = (node: Stmt): Expr | undefined =>
  node.tag === "switch" ? node.discriminant : undefined;

const withoutBreak = (
  body: ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt>
): ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt> => @{
  const last = body[body.length - 1];
  return last !== undefined && last.tag === "break"
    ? body.slice(0, -1)
    : body;
@};

export const switchToIf = (node: Stmt): Stmt => @{
  const span = node.span;
  const scrutinee = "@@switch-value";
  const target: Expr = @{ tag: "variable", name: scrutinee, span @};
  const otherwise: Stmt =
    node.tag !== "switch"
      ? @{ tag: "block", body: [], span @}
      : node.defaultBody === null
        ? @{ tag: "block", body: [], span @}
        : @{ tag: "block",
          body: withoutBreak(node.defaultBody),
          span @};
  const nest =
    node.tag !== "switch"
      ? otherwise
      : node.cases.reduceRight(
          (rest: Stmt, clause) =>
            makeIf(
              @{ tag: "binary",
                op: "===",
                left: target,
                right: clause.test,
                span @},
              @{ tag: "block", body: withoutBreak(clause.body), span @},
              rest,
              span,
            ),
          otherwise,
        );
  return @{
    tag: "block",
    body: [
      @{
        tag: "var-decl",
        kind: "const",
        name: scrutinee,
        declaredType: null,
        init: node.tag === "switch"
          ? node.discriminant
          : @{ tag: "undefined", span @},
        exported: false,
        span,
      @},
      nest,
    ],
    span,
  @};
@};
@end example

@noindent
Statements (such as the case analysis) that we choose to implement as syntactic
transformations are called @newterm{derived expressions}.  A binding form such
as @code{let} is also a derived expression (see @ref{Exercise 4.6}).@footnote{Practical
language systems provide a mechanism that allows a user
to add new derived expressions and specify their implementation as syntactic
transformations without modifying the evaluator.  Such a user-defined
transformation is called a @newterm{macro}.  Although it is easy to add an
elementary mechanism for defining macros, the resulting language has subtle
name-conflict problems.  There has been much research on mechanisms for macro
definition that do not cause these difficulties.  See, for example, @ref{Kohlbecker 1986},
@ref{Clinger and Rees 1991}, and @ref{Hanson 1991}.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.2}Exercise 4.2:} Louis Reasoner plans to reorder the
cases in @code{evaluate} so that the case for procedure
applications appears before the case for assignments.  He argues that this
will make the interpreter more efficient: Since programs usually contain more
applications than assignments, declarations, and so on, his modified
@code{evaluate}
will usually check fewer cases than the original @code{evaluate} before
identifying the type of an expression.

@enumerate a

@item
What is wrong with Louis's plan?  (Hint: What will Louis's evaluator do with
the declaration @code{const x = 3;}?)

@item
Louis is upset that his plan didn't work.  He is willing to go to any lengths
to make his evaluator recognize procedure applications before it checks for
most other kinds of expressions.  Help him by changing the syntax of the
evaluated language so that procedure applications start with @code{call}.  For
example, instead of @code{factorial(3)} we will now have to write @code{call(factorial, 3)}
and instead of @code{square(x) + 1} we will now have to write
@code{call(square, x) + 1}.

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.3}Exercise 4.3:} Rewrite @code{evaluate} so that the
dispatch is done in data-directed style.  Compare this with the data-directed
differentiation procedure of @ref{Exercise 2.73}.  (You may use the node's
@code{tag}
as the type of the expression, as is appropriate for
the syntax implemented in this section.)
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.4}Exercise 4.4:} Extend the evaluator with
strict-boolean, short-circuiting special forms @code{all} and @code{any}.
They evaluate operands from left to right, once each.  @code{all} returns
@code{false} at the first false operand and @code{true} if every operand is
true; @code{any} returns @code{true} at the first true operand and @code{false}
if every operand is false.  With no operands, @code{all} returns @code{true}
and @code{any} returns @code{false}.  A non-boolean operand produces a typed
@code{bad-operand} error.  No later operand is evaluated after a decisive
boolean.  Implement them as special forms beside the binary strict-boolean
@code{logical} node, then compare with a derived version over the conditional
expression by counting how many operand tests run.

@end quotation

@quotation
@emph{This edition replaces SICP exercise 4.5 with a host-language exercise
that keeps its number and teaching objective.}
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.5}Exercise 4.5:} The case analysis of @ref{4.1.2}
matches its discriminant against literal tests and then executes a body.  Add
an additional clause form in which, when the test matches, a
@newterm{recipient} is evaluated and then called with the matched value.  The
recipient must be a procedure of one argument, and the call's result becomes
the value of the case analysis.  For example, over the entries

@example typescript
const entries = [
  @{ key: "a", value: 1 @},
  @{ key: "b", value: 2 @},
];
@end example

@noindent
a case analysis of @code{lookup("b", entries)} with the recipient
@code{entryValue} returns 2.  Modify the handling of the case analysis
(@code{switchToIf} and the syntax procedures of @ref{4.1.2}) so that it
supports this extended clause.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.6}Exercise 4.6:} A grouped binding form is a
derived expression, because the binding node

@example typescript
type LetNode = @{
  tag: "let";
  bindings: ReadonlyArray<@{ name: string; init: Expr @}>;
  body: Block;
  span: Span;
@};
@end example

@noindent
is equivalent to a call of a function expression whose parameters are the
bound names and whose body is the group's body, applied to the initializers:

@example typescript
readAll(`
  ((⟨@var{var₁}⟩: ⟨@var{type₁}⟩,
    @r{…},
    ⟨@var{varₙ}⟩: ⟨@var{typeₙ}⟩) => @{
    ⟨@var{body}⟩
  @})(⟨@var{exp₁}⟩, @r{…}, ⟨@var{expₙ}⟩);
`)
@end example

Implement a syntactic transformation @code{letToCall} that reduces
evaluating a @code{LetNode} to evaluating a call of the type shown
above, and add the appropriate case to @code{evaluate} to handle the
binding form.  (The extension's node is rejected by the core checker until
you admit it in your own experiment, exactly as the lazy and search nodes of
@ref{4.2} and @ref{4.3} are.)
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.7}Exercise 4.7:} Sequential binding is similar to
grouped binding, except that the bindings are performed
sequentially from left to right, and each binding is made in an environment in
which all of the preceding bindings are visible.  For example, the group
binding @code{x} to 3, @code{y} to @code{x + 2}, and @code{z} to
@code{x + y + 5}, whose body is @code{x * z},
returns 39.  Explain how a sequential binding can be rewritten as a set of
nested grouped bindings, and write a procedure @code{sequentialToNested}
that performs this transformation.  If we have already implemented grouped
binding (@ref{Exercise 4.6}) and we want to extend the evaluator to handle
sequential binding,
is it sufficient to add a case to @code{evaluate} whose action is

@example typescript
evaluate(sequentialToNested(exp), env)
@end example

@noindent
or must we explicitly expand sequential binding in terms of non-derived
expressions?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.8}Exercise 4.8:} ``Named binding'' is a variant of
the grouped binding form in which the bound names are the parameters of a
local function whose body is the group's body, the function is named within
its own body, and it is called immediately on the initializers.  Thus, one can
repeatedly execute the
body by invoking the local function.  For example, the iterative Fibonacci
procedure
(@ref{1.2.2}) can be written in the guest language as follows:

@example typescript
readAll(`
  function fib(n: number): number @{
    const iter = (
      a: number,
      b: number,
      count: number,
    ): number =>
      count === 0 ? b : iter(a + b, a, count - 1);
    return iter(1, 0, n);
  @}
`)
@end example

Modify @code{letToCall} of @ref{Exercise 4.6} to also support this named
form.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.9}Exercise 4.9:} Many languages support a variety of
iteration constructs, such as @code{do}, @code{for}, @code{while}, and
@code{until}.  The guest language has @code{while} and @code{for}-@code{of},
and iterative processes can also be expressed in terms of
ordinary procedure calls, so special iteration constructs provide no essential
gain in computational power.  On the other hand, such constructs are often
convenient.  Design some iteration constructs, give examples of their use, and
show how to implement them as derived expressions over @code{while} and
@code{for}-@code{of}.
@end quotation

@quotation
