@node	4.1.3, 4.1.4, 4.1.2, 4.1
@subsection Evaluator Data Structures

In addition to defining the external syntax of expressions, the evaluator
implementation must also define the data structures that the evaluator
manipulates internally, as part of the execution of a program, such as the
representation of procedures and environments and the representation of true
and false.

@subsubheading The runtime values

Everything the evaluator reads or produces is one of the guest language's
runtime values: the JavaScript primitives, plus closures, primitive
procedures, arrays, records, maps, sets, the recursive pair and empty-list
values used for list structure, and (in the lazy experiments) thunks and
error values.

@example typescript
type Value =
  | number
  | string
  | boolean
  | null
  | undefined
  | Closure
  | Primitive
  | ArrayValue
  | RecordValue
  | MapValue
  | SetValue
  | Pair
  | Nil
  | ThunkValue
  | ErrorValue;

type Closure = @{ tag: "closure";
  params: ReadonlyArray<string>;
  rest: string | null;
  body: Block;
  env: Env @};
type Primitive = @{ tag: "primitive";
  name: string;
  fn: (args: ReadonlyArray<Value>) => Outcome @};
type ArrayValue = @{ tag: "array"; items: Value[] @};
type RecordValue = @{ tag: "record";
  fields: Map<string, Value>;
  readonlyFields: ReadonlySet<string> @};
type MapValue = @{ tag: "map"; entries: Map<Value, Value> @};
type SetValue = @{ tag: "set"; items: Set<Value> @};
type Pair = @{ tag: "cons"; head: Value; tail: Value @};
type Nil = @{ tag: "nil" @};
type ErrorValue = @{ tag: "error-value"; message: string @};
@end example

@noindent
The book's list structure is the recursive pair value: a pair is one object
with a head and a tail, the empty list is the single @code{nil} value, and a
list is finite because its tail chain must end at @code{nil}.  Thunk values
belong to the lazy experiments of @ref{4.2} and never appear in core
evaluation.

@subsubheading Testing of predicates

For conditionals, only the explicit boolean @code{true} counts as true.

@example typescript
export const isTrue = (x: Value): boolean => x === true;
@end example

@noindent
The book's @code{isFalse} is the same test negated; the edition writes
@code{!isTrue(x)} inline wherever the book writes a false test.  The guest
language requires conditions to be booleans, so the deeper question of what
counts as truth does not arise at run time; @code{evalIf} still checks,
because an externally built syntax tree can lie.

@subsubheading Representing procedures

To handle primitives, we assume that we have available the following
procedures:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{applyPrimitiveProcedure(⟨@var{proc}⟩, ⟨@var{args}⟩)}

@noindent
applies the given primitive procedure to the argument values in the array
@code{⟨}@var{args}@code{⟩} and answers the resulting @code{Outcome}.

@item
@code{isPrimitive(⟨@var{proc}⟩)}

@noindent
tests whether @code{⟨}@var{proc}@code{⟩} is a primitive procedure: in this
edition the predicate is the tag test @code{proc.tag === "primitive"}, since a
primitive is the @code{Primitive} member of the value union.

@end itemize

@noindent
These mechanisms for handling primitives are further described in
@ref{4.1.4}.

Closures are constructed from parameter lists, bodies, and
environments using the constructor @code{makeProcedure} of @ref{4.1.2}.

@noindent
The book's remaining accessors are the record itself: the book's
@code{isClosure} is the tag test @code{p.tag === "closure"}, and
@code{arrowParameters}, @code{arrowBody}, and
@code{closureEnvironment} are the fields @code{params} (with the @code{rest}
name beside it), @code{body}, and
@code{env}.

@subsubheading Operations on Environments

The evaluator needs operations for manipulating environments.  As explained in
@ref{3.2}, an environment is a sequence of frames, where each frame is
a table of bindings that associate variables with their corresponding values.
In this edition a binding pairs a name with a mutable cell, so a captured
binding is shared: a closure created before an assignment observes the new
value afterward, exactly as the environment model requires.  The cell also
records whether its name has been initialized, which is how the evaluator
implements the temporal dead zone of @code{const} and @code{let}: a read
before initialization answers a @code{tdz-access} error rather than a silent
@code{undefined}.  The cell also records whether the name may be rebound: a
@code{const} binding carries a cell with @code{mutable} false, so the
evaluator refuses the rebinding at run time just as the subset checker refuses
it statically.  We use the following operations for manipulating
environments:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{lookupVariableValue(⟨@var{name}⟩, ⟨@var{env}⟩)}

returns the value that is bound to the name @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} in the environment
@code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩}, or answers an error if the variable is unbound or
uninitialized.

@item
@code{extendEnvironment(⟨@var{procedure}⟩, ⟨@var{args}⟩)}

returns a new environment, consisting of a new frame in which the parameter
names of the closure @code{⟨}@var{procedure}@code{⟩} are bound to the corresponding
elements of the array @code{⟨}@var{args}@code{⟩}, where the enclosing environment is the
environment the closure carries.  A wrong number of arguments answers a
@code{wrong-arity} error.

@item
@code{defineVariableValue(⟨@var{name}⟩, ⟨@var{value}⟩, ⟨@var{env}⟩)}

adds to the first frame in the environment @code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩} a new binding that
associates the name @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} with the value @code{⟨}@var{value}@code{⟩}, or updates
the binding if the first frame already has one.

@item
@code{setVariableValue(⟨@var{name}⟩, ⟨@var{value}⟩, ⟨@var{env}⟩)}

changes the binding of the variable @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} in the environment @code{⟨}@var{env}@code{⟩}
so that the variable is now bound to the value @code{⟨}@var{value}@code{⟩}, or answers an
error if the variable is unbound or bound by a @code{const} declaration.

@end itemize

@noindent
To implement these operations we represent an environment as a chain of
frames: each environment pairs one frame of bindings with the environment that
encloses it.  The enclosing environment of an environment is its
@code{parent} field.  The empty environment is the @code{null}
parent at the end of the chain.

@example typescript
type Cell = @{ value: Value | undefined;
  initialized: boolean;
  mutable: boolean @};
type Binding = @{ name: string; cell: Cell @};
type Env = @{ bindings: Map<string, Cell>; parent: Env | null @};

// The book's enclosing-environment is env.parent.
// The book's first-frame is the frame map itself: env.bindings.
// The book's the-empty-environment is null: the chain ends.
@end example

@noindent
Each frame of an environment is the map described
above, and the frame procedures that shape the book's listings reduce to
writing that map.@footnote{Frames are not really a data abstraction in the
following code: @code{setVariableValue} and @code{defineVariableValue} write
through the cell, directly modifying the frame just as the
book's @code{setCar} does.  The purpose of the frame procedures is to make
the environment-manipulation procedures easy to read.}

@example typescript
// The book's make-frame is the empty map a fresh frame starts with.
export const child = (
  parent: Env | null
): Env => (@{ bindings: new Map(), parent @});

// The book's add-binding-to-frame!: one more binding in this frame.
export const addBindingToFrame = (
  name: string,
  value: Value,
  env: Env
): void => @{
  env.bindings.set(name, @{ value, initialized: true, mutable: true @});
@};
@end example

@noindent
To extend an environment by a new frame that associates parameters with
arguments, we make a frame from the closure's parameter names and the argument
array, and we
adjoin this to the environment.  We signal an error if the number of
parameters does not match the number of arguments; the edition answers a single
typed @code{wrong-arity} error where the book signals two distinct messages.  A
rest parameter collects whatever arguments remain into one array value.

@example typescript
export const extendEnvironment = (
  procedure: Closure,
  args: Value[]
): Outcome => @{
  const required = procedure.params.length;
  const hasRest = procedure.rest !== null;
  if (args.length < required || (!hasRest && args.length > required)) @{
    return fail(
        @{ tag: "wrong-arity",
        expected: required,
        given: args.length @}
      );
  @}
  const frame = child(procedure.env);
  procedure.params.forEach((name, index) => @{
    frame.bindings.set(
      name,
      @{ value: args[index],
      initialized: true,
      mutable: true @}
    );
  @});
  if (hasRest && procedure.rest !== null) @{
    frame.bindings.set(procedure.rest, @{
      value: @{ tag: "array", items: args.slice(required) @},
      initialized: true,
      mutable: true,
    @});
  @}
  return ok(frame);
@};
@end example

@noindent
To look up a variable in an environment, we look the name up in the first
frame's map.  If we find the desired variable, we return its cell.  If we do
not find the variable in the current frame, we search the enclosing
environment, and so on.  If we reach the empty environment, we answer an
``unbound name'' error.

@example typescript
// The book's scan over a frame's parallel lists becomes one map
// lookup, then a step to the parent frame on a miss.
export const findCell = (env: Env, name: string): Cell | undefined => @{
  let current: Env | null = env;
  while (current !== null) @{
    const cell = current.bindings.get(name);
    if (cell !== undefined) @{
      return cell;
    @}
    current = current.parent;
  @}
  return undefined;
@};

// The book's lookupVariableValue.
export const lookupVariableValue = (
  name: string,
  env: Env
): Outcome => @{
  const cell = findCell(env, name);
  if (cell === undefined) @{
    return fail(@{ tag: "unbound-name", name @});
  @}
  if (!cell.initialized) @{
    return fail(@{ tag: "tdz-access", name @});
  @}
  return ok(cell.value);
@};
@end example

@noindent
To set a variable to a new value in a specified environment, we look for the
variable just as in @code{lookupVariableValue}, and change the corresponding
cell when we find it, writing through the frame that defines it.

@example typescript
// The scan for setVariableValue: descend until a frame binds the
// name, then write through that binding's cell. A miss on the
// whole chain
// answers unbound-name instead of defining the name.
export const setVariableValue = (
  name: string,
  value: Value,
  env: Env
): Outcome => @{
  const cell = findCell(env, name);
  if (cell === undefined) @{
    return fail(@{ tag: "unbound-name", name @});
  @}
  if (!cell.mutable && cell.initialized) @{
    return fail(
        @{ tag: "bad-operand",
        operator: "=",
        detail: "assignment to a const binding" @}
      );
  @}
  cell.value = value;
  cell.initialized = true;
  return ok(undefined);
@};
@end example

@noindent
To define a variable, we search the first frame for a binding for the variable,
and change the binding if it exists (just as in @code{setVariableValue}).
If no such binding exists, we adjoin one to the first frame.

@example typescript
// The book's defineVariableValue: bind in this frame, shadowing
// outer ones.
export const defineVariableValue = (
  name: string,
  value: Value,
  mutable: boolean,
  env: Env,
): Outcome => @{
  const cell = env.bindings.get(name);
  if (cell === undefined) @{
    env.bindings.set(name, @{ value, initialized: true, mutable @});
  @} else @{
    cell.value = value;
    cell.initialized = true;
    cell.mutable = mutable;
  @}
  return ok(undefined);
@};
@end example

@noindent
The method described here is only one of many plausible ways to represent
environments.  Since we used data abstraction to isolate the rest of the
evaluator from the detailed choice of representation, we could change the
environment representation if we wanted to.  (See @ref{Exercise 4.11}.)  In a
production-quality language system, the speed of the evaluator's environment
operations, especially that of variable lookup, has a major impact on the
performance of the system.  The representation described here, although
conceptually simple, is not efficient and would not ordinarily be used in a
production system.@footnote{The drawback of this representation (as well as the
variant in @ref{Exercise 4.11}) is that the evaluator may have to search
through many frames in order to find the binding for a given variable.  (Such
an approach is referred to as @newterm{deep binding}.)  One way to avoid this
inefficiency is to make use of a strategy called @newterm{lexical addressing},
which will be discussed in @ref{5.5.6}.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.11}Exercise 4.11:} Instead of representing a frame
as a map, we can represent a frame as an array of name-cell bindings, searched
in order, where each
binding is a name-value pair.  Rewrite the environment operations to use this
alternative representation.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.12}Exercise 4.12:} The procedures
@code{defineVariableValue}, @code{setVariableValue} and
@code{lookupVariableValue} can be expressed in terms of more abstract
procedures for traversing the environment structure.  Define abstractions that
capture the common patterns and redefine the three procedures in terms of these
abstractions.
@end quotation

@quotation
@emph{This edition replaces SICP exercise 4.13 with a host-language exercise
that keeps its number and teaching objective.}
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.13}Exercise 4.13:} The guest language creates new
bindings for names by means of @code{const} and @code{let} declarations, but
provides no way to get
rid of bindings.  Implement for the evaluator a guest extension
@code{unbind} that removes the binding of a given name from the
environment in which the @code{unbind} call is evaluated.  This
problem is not completely specified.  For example, should we remove only the
binding in the first frame of the environment?  What should a later read of an
unbound name answer, and how does the choice interact with the temporal dead
zone of @ref{4.1.3}?  Complete the specification and
justify any choices you make.
@end quotation

@node	4.1.4, 4.1.5, 4.1.3, 4.1
@subsection Running the Evaluator as a Program

Given the evaluator, we have in our hands a description (expressed in
TypeScript) of the process by which the evaluated language's expressions are
evaluated.  One advantage of expressing the evaluator as a program is that we
can run the program.  This gives us, running within TypeScript, a working
model of how the language itself evaluates expressions.  This can serve as a
framework for experimenting with evaluation rules, as we shall do later in
this chapter.

Our evaluator program reduces expressions ultimately to the application of
primitive procedures.  Therefore, all that we need to run the evaluator is to
create a mechanism that calls on the underlying TypeScript host to model the
application of primitive procedures.

There must be a binding for each primitive procedure name, so that when
@code{evaluate} evaluates the callee of an application of a primitive, it
will find an object to pass to @code{applyProcedure}.  We thus set up a global
environment that associates unique objects with the names of the primitive
procedures that can appear in the expressions we will be evaluating.  Nothing
is bound for the booleans: @code{true} and @code{false} are literals of the
guest language, not variables, and the environment of @ref{4.1.3} binds
nothing by that name.

@example typescript
export const setupEnvironment = (): Env => @{
  const env = child(null);
  for (const procedure of makePrimitiveProcedures()) @{
    env.bindings.set(
      procedure.name,
      @{ value: procedure,
      initialized: true @}
    );
  @}
  return env;
@};

const theGlobalEnvironment = setupEnvironment();
@end example

@noindent
It does not matter how we represent the primitive procedure objects, so long
as @code{applyProcedure} can identify and apply them by reading the value's
tag and by using @code{applyPrimitiveProcedure}.  We have chosen to represent
a primitive procedure as the @code{Primitive} member of the value union: a
record carrying the primitive's name and a pure function over evaluated
arguments that answers an @code{Outcome}.

@example typescript
// The book's isPrimitiveProcedure is the union tag test:
// proc.tag === "primitive"
// The book's primitiveImplementation is that record's fn field: the
// pure function behind the primitive.
@end example

@noindent
@code{setupEnvironment} gets the primitive names and implementation functions
from the guest builtin table:@footnote{Any pure function in the admitted
subset can
be used as a primitive for the metacircular evaluator.  The name of a
primitive installed in the evaluator need not be the same as the name of the
function behind it; the names are the same here because the evaluator
implements the guest language itself.  Thus, for example, we could
install a primitive named @code{"first"} whose function is
@code{arrayFirst}, or bind the name
@code{"square"} to a host function @code{(x) => x * x}.}  The book's
@code{primitive-procedure-names} and @code{primitive-procedure-objects} are
the name and the record of each entry; this edition never builds the two
lists
separately, but binds each name to a @code{Primitive} record in the single
pass shown above.

@example typescript
const primitive = (name: string, fn: Primitive["fn"]): Primitive =>
  (@{ tag: "primitive", name, fn @});

export const makePrimitiveProcedures = (): ReadonlyArray<Primitive> => [
  primitive("arrayAt", (args) => @{
    const items = args[0];
    const index = args[1];
    if (
      items === undefined ||
      index === undefined ||
      items === null ||
      typeof items !== "object" ||
      items.tag !== "array" ||
      typeof index !== "number"
    ) @{
      return fail(
          @{ tag: "bad-operand",
          operator: "arrayAt",
          detail: "expected an array and an index" @}
        );
    @}
    const found = items.items.at(index);
    return found === undefined
      ? fail(@{ tag: "bad-operand",
        operator: "arrayAt",
        detail: "index out of range" @})
      : ok(found);
  @}),
  // ... arrayLength, sqrt, and the rest of the table follow the same
  // shape: a name plus a pure function over the evaluated arguments.
  // The driver's output operation is not a primitive: the kernel
  // returns transcript data and the boundary writes it.
];
@end example

@noindent
To apply a primitive procedure, we simply call the function stored in
the record, handing it the evaluated argument
array:@footnote{@code{applyProcedure} is the @code{apply} procedure
we have used in earlier chapters: the book's original saves the underlying
@code{apply} under that name before the metacircular evaluator defines its
own @code{apply}, which would otherwise mask it.  This edition has no such
renaming to manage: the evaluator's apply is @code{applyProcedure}, and each
primitive record already carries the function behind it, so the problem
dissolves.}

@example typescript
export const applyPrimitiveProcedure = (
  procedure: Primitive,
  args: ReadonlyArray<Value>,
): Outcome => procedure.fn(args);
@end example

@noindent
For convenience in running the metacircular evaluator, we provide a
@newterm{driver loop} that models the read-eval-print loop of the underlying
language system.  This edition runs the loop over a finite session: it takes the
global environment and the session's inputs as strings, and for each input it
reports the @newterm{prompt} line, the input source, the @newterm{output
prompt}, and the printed result, so as to distinguish the value of the
expression from other output that may be printed.@footnote{The reader
@code{read(text)} from @code{read.ts} stands in for the book's primitive
@code{read}: it parses exactly one expression out of the string it is
given and rejects trailing input; @code{readAll(text)} parses a whole
program.  For example, @code{read("x + 23")} answers a @code{binary} node
whose operator is @code{+}, with the variable node @code{x} on the left and
the number node 23 on the right.
@code{readAll("const x = 3;")} answers a one-element program whose statement
is the @code{var-decl} node for @code{x}.}

@example typescript
// The book's inputPrompt and outputPrompt are the literal lines the
// transcript reports for every input.

export const driverLoop = (
  env: Env,
  inputs: ReadonlyArray<string>,
): ReadonlyArray<string> => @{
  const transcript: string[] = [];
  for (const input of inputs) @{
    transcript.push(";;; M-Eval input:", input);
    const admitted = admitSource(input);
    if (!admitted.ok) @{
      transcript.push(
        ";;; M-Eval value:",
        formatDiagnostic(admitted.diagnostics)
      );
      continue;
    @}
    let last: Completion = @{ tag: "normal", value: undefined @};
    for (const statement of admitted.program) @{
      last = execItem(statement, env);
      if (last.tag !== "normal") @{
        break;
      @}
    @}
    transcript.push(";;; M-Eval value:", format(last));
  @}
  return transcript;
@};
@end example

@noindent
The book's @code{promptForInput} and @code{announceOutput} are the two
prompt lines in each transcript block, and the book's pair of @code{read} and
@code{eval} is the @code{admitSource} and @code{execute} pair above.  Each
input is admitted as a whole---parsed, checked against the subset, and
type-checked by the pinned TypeScript compiler---before any of its statements
executes.  A rejected input reports its diagnostics and produces no effect at
all, so a later invalid statement can never run after an earlier one has
already mutated the environment; a runtime failure reaches the transcript as
@code{Error:} followed by the @code{GuestError} the completion carries.

@noindent
We use a special printing procedure to avoid printing the environment part of
a closure, which may be a very long structure (or may even contain
cycles).  This edition's printer is @code{format} from @code{read.ts}, which
renders a closure without its environment, so no part of
the closure, environment included, ever reaches the transcript.

@example typescript
// The book's userPrint: format(value) renders a closure without the
// environment it carries, so the environment is never printed.
@end example

@noindent
Now all we need to do to run the evaluator is to initialize the global
environment and start the driver loop.  Here is a sample interaction:

@example typescript
const theGlobalEnvironment = setupEnvironment();

const transcript = driverLoop(theGlobalEnvironment, [
  `
  function append(items: number[], more: number[]): number[] @{
    const out: number[] = [];
    for (const item of items) @{
      out.push(item);
    @}
    for (const item of more) @{
      out.push(item);
    @}
    return out;
  @}
  `,
  "append([1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]);",
]);
// ;;; M-Eval input:
// function append(items: number[], more: number[]): number[] @{ @dots{}
// @}
// ;;; M-Eval value:
// undefined
// ;;; M-Eval input:
// append([1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]);
// ;;; M-Eval value:
// [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.14}Exercise 4.14:} Eva Lu Ator and Louis Reasoner
are each experimenting with the metacircular evaluator.  Eva types in the
guest definition of @code{map}, and runs some test programs that use it.  They work
fine.  Louis, in contrast, has installed the host's own version of @code{map} as a
primitive for the metacircular evaluator.  When he tries it, things go terribly
wrong.  Explain why Louis's @code{map} fails even though Eva's works.
@end quotation
