binding in place of
the recursive binding in the definition of @code{f}.

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.21}Exercise 4.21:} Amazingly, Louis's intuition in
@ref{Exercise 4.20} is correct.  It is indeed possible to specify recursive
procedures without using a recursive binding (or even an internal declaration), although the
method for accomplishing this is much more subtle than Louis imagined.  The
following program computes 10 factorial by applying a recursive factorial
procedure that receives itself as an argument:@footnote{This example illustrates
a programming trick for
formulating recursive procedures without using declarations.  The most general
trick of this sort is the @math{Y} @newterm{operator}, which can be used to give a
``pure λ-calculus'' implementation of recursion.  (See @ref{Stoy 1977} for
details on the λ-calculus, and @ref{Gabriel 1988} for an exposition of the
@math{Y} operator.)}

@example typescript
readAll(`
  interface Step @{
    run: (self: Step, k: number) => number;
  @}

  const factorialStep: Step = @{
    run: (self, k) =>
      k <= 1 ? 1 : k * self.run(self, k - 1),
  @};

  const applyStep = (
    step: Step,
    n: number
  ): number => step.run(step, n);

  applyStep(factorialStep, 10);
`)
@end example

@enumerate a

@item
Check (by evaluating the program) that this really does compute factorials.
Devise an analogous program for computing Fibonacci numbers.

@item
Consider the following procedure, which includes mutually recursive internal
declarations:

@example typescript
readAll(`
  function f(x: number): boolean @{
    function even(n: number): boolean @{
      return n === 0 ? true : odd(n - 1);
    @}
    function odd(n: number): boolean @{
      return n === 0 ? false : even(n - 1);
    @}
    return even(x);
  @}
`)
@end example

Fill in the missing expressions to complete an alternative definition of
@code{f}, which uses neither internal declarations nor recursive bindings:

