@subsection Separating Syntactic Analysis from Execution

The evaluator implemented above is simple, but it is very inefficient, because
the syntactic analysis of expressions is interleaved with their execution.
Thus if a program is executed many times, its syntax is analyzed many times.
Consider, for example, evaluating @code{factorial(4)} using the following
declaration of @code{factorial}:

@example typescript
const factorialProgram = readAll(`
function factorial(n: number): number @{
  return n <= 1 ? 1 : factorial(n - 1) * n;
@}
`);
@end example

@noindent
Each time @code{factorial} is called, the evaluator must determine that the
body is a @code{return} statement and extract its operand.  Only then can it
evaluate the operand and dispatch on its value.  Each time it evaluates the
expression @code{factorial(n - 1) * n}, or the subexpressions
@code{factorial(n - 1)} and @code{n - 1}, the evaluator must perform the
case analysis in @code{evaluate} to determine that the expression is a
call or a binary operation, and must extract its callee and arguments.  This
analysis is
expensive.  Performing it repeatedly is wasteful.

We can transform the evaluator to be significantly more efficient by arranging
things so that syntactic analysis is performed only once.@footnote{This
technique is an integral part of the compilation process, which we shall
discuss in @ref{Chapter 5}.  Jonathan Rees wrote an interpreter like this
in about 1982 for the T project (@ref{Rees and Adams 1982}).  Marc @ref{Feeley (1986)} (see
also @ref{Feeley and Lapalme 1987}) independently invented this technique in his
master's thesis.} We split @code{evaluate}, which takes an expression and an
environment, into two parts.  The procedure @code{analyze} takes only the
expression.  It performs the syntactic analysis and returns a new procedure,
the @newterm{execution procedure}, that encapsulates the work to be done in
executing the analyzed expression.  The execution procedure takes an
environment as its argument and completes the evaluation.  This saves work
because @code{analyze} will be called only once on an expression, while the
execution procedure may be called many times.

In this edition an execution procedure is a function of type
@code{ExecutionProcedure}: it takes the environment and answers the
@code{Outcome} that carries out the analyzed expression's remaining work:

@example typescript
export type ExecutionProcedure = (env: Env) => Outcome;
@end example

With the separation into analysis and execution, @code{evalAnalyzed} now becomes

@example typescript
export const evalAnalyzed = (
  expr: Expr,
  env: Env
): Outcome => analyze(expr)(env);
@end example

@noindent
The result of calling @code{analyze} is the execution procedure to be applied
to the environment.  The @code{analyze} procedure is the same case analysis as
performed by the original @code{evaluate} of @ref{4.1.1}, except that the
procedures to which we dispatch perform only analysis, not full evaluation:

@example typescript
export const analyze = (expr: Expr): ExecutionProcedure => @{
  switch (expr.tag) @{
    case "number":
    case "string":
    case "boolean":
    case "null":
    case "undefined":
      return analyzeSelfEvaluating(expr);
    case "variable":
      return analyzeVariable(expr.name);
    case "template":
      return analyzeTemplate(expr);
    case "array":
      return analyzeArray(expr);
    case "object":
      return analyzeObject(expr);
    case "unary":
      return analyzeUnary(expr);
    case "binary":
      return analyzeBinary(expr);
    case "logical":
      return analyzeLogical(expr);
    case "conditional":
      return analyzeConditional(expr);
    case "assign":
      return analyzeAssignment(expr.target, expr.value);
    case "arrow":
      return analyzeLambda(expr);
    case "call":
      return analyzeApplication(expr);
    case "member":
    case "index":
      return analyzeAccess(expr);
    case "new-error":
    case "new-map":
    case "new-set":
      return analyzeConstruction(expr);
    case "delay":
    case "force":
    case "choose":
    case "require":
    case "ramb":
      return () => fail(@{ tag: "unknown-syntax",
        construct: expr.tag @});
  @}
@};
@end example

@noindent
Here is the simplest syntactic analysis procedure, which handles
self-evaluating expressions.  It returns an execution procedure that ignores
its environment argument and just returns the expression's value:

@example typescript
export const analyzeSelfEvaluating =
  (expr: Expr): ExecutionProcedure =>
  () =>
    ok(literalValue(expr));
@end example

@noindent
For a data literal, we can gain a little efficiency by analyzing the element
expressions only once, in the analysis phase, rather than in the execution
phase:

@example typescript
export const analyzeArray = (expr: Expr): ExecutionProcedure => @{
  const elements = expr.tag === "array"
    ? expr.elements.map((arg) => analyze(arg.expr))
    : [];
  return (env) => @{
    const items: Value[] = [];
    for (const element of elements) @{
      const value = element(env);
      if (value.tag !== "ok") @{
        return value;
      @}
      items.push(value.value);
    @}
    return ok(@{ tag: "array", items @});
  @};
@};
@end example

@noindent
Looking up a variable value must still be done in the execution phase, since
this depends upon knowing the environment.@footnote{There is, however, an
important part of the variable search that @emph{can} be done as part of the
syntactic analysis.  As we will show in @ref{5.5.6}, one can determine
the position in the environment structure where the value of the variable will
be found, thus obviating the need to scan the environment for the entry that
matches the variable.}

@example typescript
export const analyzeVariable =
  (name: string): ExecutionProcedure =>
  (env) =>
    lookupVariableValue(name, env);
@end example

@noindent
@code{analyzeAssignment} also must defer actually setting the variable until
the execution, when the environment has been supplied.  However, the fact that
the assigned expression can be analyzed (recursively) during
analysis is a major gain in efficiency, because that
expression will now be analyzed only once.  The same holds true for
declarations.

@example typescript
export const analyzeAssignment = (
  target: Expr,
  valueExpr: Expr
): ExecutionProcedure => @{
  const vproc = analyze(valueExpr);
  return (env) => @{
    const value = vproc(env);
    if (value.tag !== "ok") @{
      return value;
    @}
    return target.tag === "variable"
      ? setVariableValue(target.name, value.value, env)
      : memberSet(target, value.value, env);
  @};
@};

// The statement-side twin, answering a Completion like evalDeclaration:
type StatementProcedure = (env: Env) => Completion;

export const analyzeDeclaration = (
  name: string,
  initializer: ExecutionProcedure,
): StatementProcedure =>
  (env) => @{
    const value = initializer(env);
    if (value.tag !== "ok") @{
      return @{ tag: "error", error: value.error @};
    @}
    const installed = defineVariableValue(name, value.value, env);
    return installed.tag === "ok"
      ? @{ tag: "normal", value: undefined @}
      : @{ tag: "error", error: installed.error @};
  @};
@end example

@noindent
For @code{if} statements, we extract and analyze the test, consequent,
and alternative at analysis time.

@example typescript
export const analyzeIf = (
  test: Expr,
  consequent: Stmt,
  alternative: Stmt | null,
): StatementProcedure => @{
  const pproc = analyze(test);
  const cproc = analyzeStatement(consequent);
  const aproc = alternative === null
    ? undefined
    : analyzeStatement(alternative);
  return (env) => @{
    const predicate = pproc(env);
    if (predicate.tag !== "ok") @{
      return @{ tag: "error", error: predicate.error @};
    @}
    if (typeof predicate.value !== "boolean") @{
      return @{
        tag: "error",
        error: @{ tag: "bad-operand",
        operator: "if",
        detail: "test is not a boolean" @},
      @};
    @}
    if (predicate.value) @{
      return cproc(env);
    @}
    return aproc === undefined
      ? @{ tag: "normal", value: undefined @}
      : aproc(env);
  @};
@};
@end example

@noindent
Analyzing a function expression also achieves a major gain in efficiency:
We analyze the body only once, even though procedures resulting
from evaluation of the function may be applied many times.  The analyzed
evaluator therefore prepares the statement procedure of the body in a
module-local analysis cache keyed by the body block, and procedure creation
answers the ordinary landed @code{Closure} of @ref{4.1.3}---parameter names,
the body block, and the environment.  (The cache and the prepared-body
application below are presentation helpers of this section, not engine
exports; the landed engine dispatches statements through @code{execStatement}
and applications through @code{callClosure}.)

@example typescript
const analyzedBodies = new Map<Stmt, StatementProcedure>();

const bodyProcedure = (body: Stmt): StatementProcedure => @{
  const cached = analyzedBodies.get(body);
  if (cached !== undefined) return cached;
  const prepared = analyzeStatement(body);
  analyzedBodies.set(body, prepared);
  return prepared;
@};

const analyzeLambda = (
  params: ReadonlyArray<Param>,
  body: Block,
): ExecutionProcedure => @{
  const names: string[] = [];
  let rest: string | null = null;
  for (const param of params) @{
    if (param.kind === "param") @{
      names.push(param.name);
    @} else @{
      rest = param.name;
    @}
  @}
  return (env) => ok(makeClosure(names, rest, body, env));
@};
@end example

@noindent
Analysis of a sequence of statements (a block or the body of a
function expression) is more involved.@footnote{See @ref{Exercise 4.23}
for some insight into the processing of sequences.} Each statement in the
sequence is analyzed, yielding a statement procedure.  These statement
procedures are combined to produce a statement procedure that takes
an environment as argument and sequentially calls each individual
procedure with the environment as argument.

@example typescript
const sequentially =
  (proc1: StatementProcedure,
    proc2: StatementProcedure): StatementProcedure =>
  (env) => @{
    const first = proc1(env);
    return first.tag === "normal" ? proc2(env) : first;
  @};

const analyzeSequence = (
  statements: ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt>,
): StatementProcedure => @{
  let combined: StatementProcedure = () => @{ tag: "normal",
    value: undefined @};
  for (let index = statements.length - 1;
    index >= 0;
    index = index - 1) @{
    const statement = statements[index];
    if (statement !== undefined) @{
      combined = sequentially(analyzeStatement(statement), combined);
    @}
  @}
  return combined;
@};
@end example

@noindent
To analyze an application, we analyze the callee and arguments and construct
an execution procedure that calls the callee execution procedure (to obtain
the actual procedure to be applied) and the argument execution procedures (to
obtain the actual arguments).  We then pass these to
@code{executeApplication}, which is the analog of @code{applyProcedure} in
@ref{4.1.1}.  @code{executeApplication} differs from @code{applyProcedure} in that the
procedure body for a closure has already been analyzed, so there is
no need to do further analysis.  Instead, we just call the prepared
procedure
for the body on the extended environment.

@example typescript
export const analyzeApplication = (
  callee: Expr,
  args: ReadonlyArray<Arg>,
): ExecutionProcedure => @{
  const fproc = analyze(callee);
  const aprocs = args.map((arg) => analyze(arg.expr));
  return (env) => @{
    const procedure = fproc(env);
    if (procedure.tag !== "ok") @{
      return procedure;
    @}
    const values = runOperands(aprocs, env);
    if (values.tag !== "ok") @{
      return values;
    @}
    return executeApplication(procedure.value, values.value);
  @};
@};

const runOperands = (
  aprocs: ReadonlyArray<ExecutionProcedure>,
  env: Env
): Outcome => @{
  const args: Value[] = [];
  for (const aproc of aprocs) @{
    const value = aproc(env);
    if (value.tag !== "ok") @{
      return value;
    @}
    args.push(value.value);
  @}
  return ok(args);
@};

const executeApplication = (
  procedure: Value,
  args: Value[]
): Outcome => @{
  if (isPrimitive(procedure)) @{
    return applyPrimitiveProcedure(procedure, args);
  @}
  if (isClosure(procedure)) @{
    const frame = extendEnvironment(procedure, args);
    if (frame.tag !== "ok") @{
      return frame;
    @}
    const completion = bodyProcedure(procedure.body)(frame.value);
    return executeProgram(completion);
  @}
  return fail(@{ tag: "not-callable", detail: format(procedure) @});
@};
@end example

@noindent
The @code{executeProgram} helper is the same fold @code{applyProcedure}
of @ref{4.1.1} performs: a normal or returned completion becomes the call's
value, an uncaught guest throw becomes @code{guest-throw}, and a stray
@code{break} or @code{continue} is reported.

@noindent
Our new evaluator uses the same data structures, syntax procedures, and
run-time support procedures as in @ref{4.1.2}, @ref{4.1.3}, and
@ref{4.1.4}.

