@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 4.23}Exercise 4.23:} Alyssa P. Hacker doesn't
understand why @code{analyzeSequence} needs to be so complicated.  All the
other analysis procedures are straightforward transformations of the
corresponding evaluation procedures (or @code{evaluate} cases) in
@ref{4.1.1}.  She expected @code{analyzeSequence} to look like this:

@example typescript
const analyzeSequenceAlyssa = (
  statements: ReadonlyArray<Decl | Stmt>,
): StatementProcedure => @{
  const procs = statements.map(analyzeStatement);
  const executeSequence = (
    inner: ReadonlyArray<StatementProcedure>,
    env: Env,
  ): Completion => @{
    const first = inner[0];
    if (first === undefined) @{
      return @{ tag: "normal", value: undefined @};
    @}
    const firstCompletion = first(env);
    return firstCompletion.tag === "normal" &&
      inner.length > 1
      ? executeSequence(inner.slice(1), env)
      : firstCompletion;
  @};
  return (env) => executeSequence(procs, env);
@};
@end example

Eva Lu Ator explains to Alyssa that the version in the text does more of the
work of evaluating a sequence at analysis time.  Alyssa's sequence-execution
procedure, rather than having the calls to the individual execution procedures
built in, loops through the procedures in order to call them: In effect,
although the individual statements in the sequence have been analyzed, the
sequence itself has not been.

Compare the two versions of @code{analyzeSequence}.  For example, consider the
common case (typical of procedure bodies) where the sequence has just one
statement.  What work will the execution procedure produced by Alyssa's
program do?  What about the execution procedure produced by the program in the
text above?  How do the two versions compare for a sequence with two
statements?
@end quotation

